+# LIFE-394 live captures (codex 0.155.1) showed two reasons this rule could never fire on a real
+# launch, and — once those were fixed — a third reason it fired where it must not:
+#   1. the header is drawn inside a box, so the line is `>-You are in <cwd>---\u{256e}` (U+2500 after `>`),
+#      not `> You are in`;
+#   2. the detection text of a launched pane begins with the shell line that started codex
+#      (`sh-3.2$ codex -m ...`), so `\A` never reached the header at all;
+#   3. with the anchor moved to a line start, a multi-line transcript quote of the same header and
+#      question matched too (review r3 fixture `codex-multiline-quote-idle`).
+# So the rule also requires the live chooser's own control lines — the two numbered options on
+# consecutive rows. Live capture:
+#   >-You are in /private/tmp/.../repo---\u{256e}
+#
+#     Do you trust the contents of this directory? Working with untrusted contents comes with ...
+#
+#   \u{203a} 1. Yes, continue
+#     2. No, quit
+#
+#     Press enter to continue
+#
+# The `not` gate below is what separates a live chooser from a quotation of one. Review r4 showed
+# that a plain "the marker appears anywhere in the region" test is wrong in both directions: a cwd
+# that happens to contain the composer placeholder (`.../ask codex to do anything`) silenced a real
+# chooser. So the gate matches the composer placeholder only as a **whole line** and only **after**
+# the chooser's own option rows — that is the one arrangement a quotation produces and a live
+# chooser cannot.
+#
+# LIFE-603 K1: codex-cli 0.162.1 replaced that chooser with a new screen, so the rule now has two
+# branches under `any`, each with its own control lines and its own quotation gate. The 0.155.1
+# branch is unchanged. The 0.162.1 live captures (final gate g31, coordinator x06, review r5 r05/r09):
+#     Folder access                         Folder access
+#     <cwd>                                 <cwd>
+#
+#     Note: You’re in a subdirectory ...    Config, hooks, and exec policies from untrusted
+#     <repo root>                           folders stay disabled. ...
+#
+#     Trust this folder? Codex can ...
+#
+#   › 1. Trust and continue               › 1. Open restricted
+#     2. Back to Agent Command Center       2. Back to Agent Command Center
+#
+#     enter continue · esc back             enter continue · esc back
+#
+# The second screen is what codex shows for a folder it has recorded as untrusted.
+#
+# LIFE-603 K1b (review r5 R5-A): K1 matched only the first screen's wording, so the second one read as
+# Idle and the draft went into the dialog again. The codex binary also carries `Open existing task`
+# and `Keep current directory` labels for the same screen family. So the 0.162.1 branch matches the
+# family, not its wording: the `Folder access` title as a whole line, followed later by numbered
+# option rows `1.` and `2.` on consecutive lines (a cursor glyph allowed on either), whatever the
+# labels and prose say.
+#
+# The `enter continue · esc back` footer is on every capture but is NOT required. Requiring it adds
+# a fail-open path and removes none: the region is the top 20 non-empty lines and the footer is the
+# last row, so a long prose block or a narrow pane can push it out, and a reworded footer would read
+# as Idle again. The title line plus the consecutive option rows already exclude every known
+# non-chooser screen.
+#
+# The `not` gate separates a quotation from the live chooser by what follows the option rows. A live
+# chooser has only the footer below them; a quotation is followed by the composer, whose prompt row
+# starts with a cursor glyph. So the gate fires on any cursor-led row after the option rows that is
+# not itself an option row — the placeholder `› Ask Codex to do anything` (K1) and also a composer
+# holding text (review r5 R5-B, which K1's placeholder-only gate read as Blocked). A cwd carrying
+# `Folder access` is a path row, never a whole title line.
+any = [
+  { all = [
+      { regex = ['(?m)^>[ \x{2500}]You are in [^\r\n]+(?:\r?\n|$)'] },
+      { regex = ['(?s)Do\s+you\s+trust\s+the\s+contents\s+of\s+this\s+directory\?'] },
+      { regex = ['(?m)^\s*(?:[\x{203a}\x{276f}\x{258c}>]\s+)?1\.\s+Yes,\s+continue\s*\r?\n\s*(?:[\x{203a}\x{276f}\x{258c}>]\s+)?2\.\s+No,\s+quit\s*$'] },
+    ], not = [
